{"black_box_warning":{"current_value":"No current FDA/DailyMed label identified.","fda_source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf","https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-safety-and-availability/fda-drug-safety-communication-fda-determines-2013-labeling-adequate-manage-risk-retinal"],"proposed_value":"Boxed warning in last FDA-approved Potiga label: retinal abnormalities and potential vision loss.","rationale":"The 2016 FDA Potiga label contains a boxed warning for retinal abnormalities and potential vision loss, and FDA's 2015 Drug Safety Communication explicitly refers to recommendations in the Boxed Warning. DailyMed should not be cited for this field.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect"},"blocking_questions":[],"fact_checks":[{"current_value":"ezogabine","field":"generic_name","proposed_value":"ezogabine","rationale":"FDA labeling, FDA safety communication, and NCBI LiverTox identify Potiga as ezogabine.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548304/"]},{"current_value":"retigabine","field":"alternate_generic_names","proposed_value":"retigabine","rationale":"NCBI LiverTox states ezogabine is known as retigabine in Europe, and ILAE/GSK notes the same medicine is called Potiga/ezogabine in the United States and Trobalt/retigabine elsewhere. This supports treating retigabine as an alias, not a duplicate row.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548304/","https://www.ilae.org/files/dmfile/GSK_RetigabineTrobalt-Reminder.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Potiga; Trobalt","field":"trade_names","proposed_value":"Potiga; Trobalt","rationale":"NCBI LiverTox identifies Potiga in the United States and Trobalt in Europe/elsewhere; the ILAE/GSK discontinuation notice uses Trobalt/Potiga for the same medicine.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548304/","https://www.ilae.org/files/dmfile/GSK_RetigabineTrobalt-Reminder.pdf"]},{"current_value":"retigabine","field":"pubmed_search_aliases","proposed_value":"retigabine","rationale":"The RCT literature uses retigabine and ezogabine/retigabine naming; retigabine should remain a PubMed search alias.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27376872/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21451152/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20944074/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17420403/"]},{"current_value":"No - formerly FDA-approved/marketed; withdrawn/discontinued","field":"available_in_us","proposed_value":"No - formerly FDA-approved/marketed; withdrawn/discontinued","rationale":"ILAE/GSK states Potiga/ezogabine in the United States would be discontinued on the same June 2017 timeline as Trobalt; FDA labeling confirms historical approval.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.ilae.org/files/dmfile/GSK_RetigabineTrobalt-Reminder.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Focal","field":"epilepsy_type","proposed_value":"Focal","rationale":"The FDA label indication is partial-onset seizures, the older term corresponding to focal seizures.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/overview/trobalt-epar-summary-public_en.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Tablet","field":"formulations_available","proposed_value":"Tablet","rationale":"FDA labeling lists Potiga tablets in 50, 200, 300, and 400 mg strengths; EMA lists Trobalt tablets.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/overview/trobalt-epar-summary-public_en.pdf"]},{"current_value":"7-11 h","field":"half_life_range","proposed_value":"7-11 h","rationale":"FDA labeling states ezogabine and its N-acetyl metabolite have elimination half-lives of 7 to 11 hours.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Liver","field":"major_organ_for_metabolism","proposed_value":"Liver","rationale":"FDA labeling and NCBI LiverTox describe extensive metabolism by glucuronidation and acetylation; renal excretion is the major elimination route, but the metabolism field is supported as hepatic/liver.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548304/"]},{"current_value":"Not a major CYP inducer/inhibitor","field":"enzyme_inducing_or_inhibiting","proposed_value":"Not a major CYP inducer/inhibitor","rationale":"FDA labeling states in vitro studies showed no oxidative metabolism by CYP enzymes and CYP inhibitors/inducers are unlikely to affect ezogabine/NAMR pharmacokinetics; LiverTox states metabolism is independent of microsomal P450 and lacks significant drug interactions.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548304/"]},{"current_value":"Inhibitor","field":"filter_enzyme_effect","proposed_value":"None/Not a major inducer or inhibitor","rationale":"The row's filter conflicts with the populated enzyme fact and FDA/NCBI sources; ezogabine is not a major CYP inhibitor.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548304/"]},{"current_value":"1200 mg/day","field":"maximum_approved_daily_dose","proposed_value":"1200 mg/day","rationale":"FDA label maximum general adult dosage is 400 mg three times daily, 1,200 mg/day.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"600 mg/day","field":"minimum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"600 mg/day","rationale":"FDA label maintenance dosage begins at 600 mg/day; FDA statistical review notes 600 mg/day was statistically superior to placebo in Study 302.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/nda/2011/022345Orig1s000StatR.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Withdrawn; historical adult dose 600-1200 mg/day divided TID","field":"typical_doses_per_day","proposed_value":"Withdrawn; historical adult dose 600-1200 mg/day divided TID","rationale":"FDA labeling supports 600-1,200 mg/day in three divided doses; ILAE/GSK supports withdrawal/discontinuation.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf","https://www.ilae.org/files/dmfile/GSK_RetigabineTrobalt-Reminder.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Neuronal potassium-channel opener; primarily activates Kv7/KCNQ channels, stabilizing resting membrane potential and reducing neuronal excitability.","field":"mechanism_of_action","proposed_value":"Neuronal potassium-channel opener; primarily activates Kv7/KCNQ channels, stabilizing resting membrane potential and reducing neuronal excitability.","rationale":"EMA explains retigabine keeps neuronal potassium channels open to prevent transmission of electrical impulses; Sills/Rogawski classify retigabine/ezogabine as a voltage-gated potassium-channel modulator.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/overview/trobalt-epar-summary-public_en.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32120063/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548304/"]},{"current_value":"EMA Trobalt withdrawal page; Sills and Rogawski 2020 ASM mechanism review","field":"mechanism_source","proposed_value":"EMA Trobalt EPAR summary; Sills and Rogawski 2020 ASM mechanism review; NCBI LiverTox Ezogabine","rationale":"The EMA withdrawal page supports authorization withdrawal but not mechanism. EMA's EPAR summary, Sills/Rogawski, and LiverTox support the potassium-channel mechanism.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/overview/trobalt-epar-summary-public_en.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32120063/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548304/"]},{"current_value":"High","field":"mechanism_confidence","proposed_value":"High","rationale":"Mechanism is consistently supported by EMA, NCBI LiverTox, and peer-reviewed review literature.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/overview/trobalt-epar-summary-public_en.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32120063/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548304/"]},{"current_value":"EMA/UK SmPC","field":"mechanism_source_tier","proposed_value":"EMA EPAR/peer-reviewed review","rationale":"The row cites an EMA EPAR-type source and a peer-reviewed mechanism review, not a UK SmPC located during audit.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/overview/trobalt-epar-summary-public_en.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32120063/"]},{"current_value":"CNS: dizziness 23%, CNS: somnolence 22%, constitutional: fatigue 15%, confusional state 9%, urologic: urinary retention 2%","field":"adverse_symptoms_percentages","proposed_value":"CNS: dizziness 23%, CNS: somnolence 22%, constitutional: fatigue 15%, confusional state 9%, urologic: urinary retention approximately 2%","rationale":"FDA label supports dizziness 23%, somnolence 22%, fatigue 15%, confusional state 9%, and urinary retention in approximately 2% of the epilepsy safety database. Minor wording change clarifies that urinary retention is approximate and from a broader database, not the Table 4 all-dose column.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"QT prolongation not typical; urinary/retinal toxicity drove withdrawal","field":"qt_interval_effect","proposed_value":"QT prolongation possible; FDA label reports mean 7.7-msec QT prolongation at 400 mg TID and recommends monitoring in at-risk patients. Global discontinuation was attributed by GSK/EMA to limited use/commercial reasons, not efficacy or safety.","rationale":"FDA labeling documents a QT interval effect. The withdrawal-causation statement is contradicted by ILAE/GSK and EMA withdrawal notices, which state permanent discontinuation/withdrawal was for limited usage/commercial reasons.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf","https://www.ilae.org/files/dmfile/GSK_RetigabineTrobalt-Reminder.pdf","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/public-statement/public-statement-trobalt-withdrawal-marketing-authorisation-european-union_en.pdf"]},{"current_value":"QT prolongation","field":"filter_qt_effect","proposed_value":"QT prolongation","rationale":"FDA labeling supports a QT interval effect, including a mean 7.7-msec QT prolongation at 400 mg TID.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"2011 FDA approval; EMA Trobalt/retigabine marketing authorisation granted 2011 and withdrawn 2018","field":"year_fda_cleared","proposed_value":"2011 FDA approval; EMA Trobalt/retigabine marketing authorisation granted 28 Mar 2011 and withdrawn 19 Jul 2018","rationale":"The current value is directionally correct; exact EMA dates improve precision.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/public-statement/public-statement-trobalt-withdrawal-marketing-authorisation-european-union_en.pdf"]},{"current_value":"NCBI LiverTox anticonvulsants table; EMA Trobalt withdrawal page; ILAE/GSK discontinuation notice; Sills and Rogawski 2020 ASM mechanism review; FDA/DailyMed labeling","field":"evidence_sources","proposed_value":"FDA Potiga prescribing information; FDA Potiga Drug Safety Communication; NCBI LiverTox Ezogabine; EMA Trobalt EPAR summary and withdrawal statement; ILAE/GSK Trobalt/Potiga discontinuation notice; Sills and Rogawski 2020 ASM mechanism review; cited PubMed RCTs","rationale":"DailyMed should not be used for black-box verification, and the mechanism is supported by EMA EPAR rather than the EMA withdrawal page. FDA labeling and FDA safety communication should be named explicitly.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf","https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-safety-and-availability/fda-drug-safety-communication-fda-determines-2013-labeling-adequate-manage-risk-retinal","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548304/","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/overview/trobalt-epar-summary-public_en.pdf","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/public-statement/public-statement-trobalt-withdrawal-marketing-authorisation-european-union_en.pdf","https://www.ilae.org/files/dmfile/GSK_RetigabineTrobalt-Reminder.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32120063/"]}],"generic_name":"ezogabine","missing_rcts":[],"outcome_checks":[{"current_value":"17-31 % (drug minus placebo RR50 differential at maximum effective dose/regimen: Lim2016 ezogabine/retigabine 600 mg/day 31%; French2011 ezogabine/retigabine 1200 mg/day 26.6%; Brodie2010 ezogabine/retigabine 900 mg/day 28.1%; Porter2007 retigabine 1200 mg/day 17%)","field":"diff_50_responder_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"17-31 % (drug minus placebo RR50 differential at maximum effective dose/regimen: Lim2016 ezogabine/retigabine 600 mg/day 31%; French2011 ezogabine/retigabine 1200 mg/day 26.6%; Brodie2010 ezogabine/retigabine 900 mg/day 28.1%; Porter2007 retigabine 1200 mg/day 17%)","rationale":"Differentials match active minus placebo responder rates in the cited RCT evidence: Lim 31-0=31; French 44.4-17.8=26.6; Brodie 47.0-18.9=28.1; Porter 33-16=17. FDA statistical review also supports pivotal trial designs and Brodie/Porter/French outcome patterns.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27376872/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21451152/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20944074/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17420403/","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/nda/2011/022345Orig1s000StatR.pdf"]},{"current_value":"11.69-26.8 % (drug minus placebo MPC differential at maximum effective dose/regimen: Lim2016 ezogabine/retigabine 600 mg/day 11.69%; French2011 ezogabine/retigabine 1200 mg/day 26.8%; Brodie2010 ezogabine/retigabine 900 mg/day 24%; Porter2007 retigabine 1200 mg/day 22%)","field":"diff_median_pct_change_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"11.69-26.8 % (drug minus placebo MPC differential at maximum effective dose/regimen: Lim2016 ezogabine/retigabine 600 mg/day 11.69%; French2011 ezogabine/retigabine 1200 mg/day 26.8%; Brodie2010 ezogabine/retigabine 900 mg/day 24%; Porter2007 retigabine 1200 mg/day 22%)","rationale":"Differentials match active minus placebo median percent seizure-frequency reductions in the cited evidence: French 44.3-17.5=26.8; Brodie 39.9-15.9=24.0; Porter 35.2-13.1 approximately 22.1; Lim local audit values 33.9-22.21=11.69.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27376872/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21451152/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20944074/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17420403/","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/nda/2011/022345Orig1s000StatR.pdf"]},{"current_value":"NR/not extractable as a drug-minus-placebo seizure-freedom patient-rate differential at the maximum effective dose/regimen from included phase II/III placebo-controlled RCT records","field":"diff_seizure_freedom_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"NR/not extractable as a drug-minus-placebo seizure-freedom patient-rate differential at the maximum effective dose/regimen from included phase II/III placebo-controlled RCT abstract records","rationale":"No consistent maximum-dose drug-minus-placebo seizure-freedom differential was present in the included PubMed abstract/outcome rows. FDA review notes seizure freedom was a secondary variable, but the opened FDA review lines did not provide a complete extractable row-level differential series.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27376872/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21451152/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20944074/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17420403/","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/nda/2011/022345Orig1s000StatR.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Lim2016|31|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27376872/|75; French2011|26.6|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21451152/|305; Brodie2010|28.1|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20944074/|538; Porter2007|17|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17420403/|399","field":"plot_diff_50_responder_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"Lim2016|31|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27376872/|75; French2011|26.6|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21451152/|305; Brodie2010|28.1|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20944074/|538; Porter2007|17|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17420403/|399","rationale":"Plot values and sample sizes match the retained RCT/outcome evidence and FDA review context.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27376872/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21451152/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20944074/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17420403/","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/nda/2011/022345Orig1s000StatR.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Lim2016|11.69|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27376872/|75; French2011|26.8|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21451152/|305; Brodie2010|24|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20944074/|538; Porter2007|22|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17420403/|399","field":"plot_diff_median_pct_change_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"Lim2016|11.69|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27376872/|75; French2011|26.8|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21451152/|305; Brodie2010|24|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20944074/|538; Porter2007|22|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17420403/|399","rationale":"Plot values match calculated drug-minus-placebo median percent-change differentials from the cited RCT/outcome evidence.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27376872/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21451152/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20944074/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17420403/","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/nda/2011/022345Orig1s000StatR.pdf"]},{"current_value":"","field":"plot_diff_seizure_freedom_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"","rationale":"No plotted seizure-freedom differential is present; this is consistent with the NR seizure-freedom summary.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"not_applicable","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27376872/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21451152/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20944074/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17420403/"]}],"overall_status":"needs_update","proposed_row_updates":[{"current_value":"No current FDA/DailyMed label identified.","field":"fda_black_box_warning","proposed_value":"Boxed warning in last FDA-approved Potiga label: retinal abnormalities and potential vision loss.","reason":"Directly contradicted by the 2016 FDA Potiga label and FDA safety communication.","requires_user_approval":true,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf","https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-safety-and-availability/fda-drug-safety-communication-fda-determines-2013-labeling-adequate-manage-risk-retinal"]},{"current_value":"FDA/DailyMed search on 05-20-2026: no current label found for terms [ezogabine; retigabine; Potiga; Trobalt].","field":"fda_black_box_warning_source","proposed_value":"FDA Potiga prescribing information, May 2016, and FDA Drug Safety Communication, 06-16-2015: boxed warning for retinal abnormalities and potential vision loss identified for Potiga (ezogabine).","reason":"DailyMed is not permissible for black-box verification and FDA sources identify a boxed warning.","requires_user_approval":true,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf","https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-safety-and-availability/fda-drug-safety-communication-fda-determines-2013-labeling-adequate-manage-risk-retinal"]},{"current_value":"05-20-2026","field":"fda_black_box_warning_verified","proposed_value":"05-21-2026","reason":"Use the actual audit verification date for the corrected FDA boxed-warning finding.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Inhibitor","field":"filter_enzyme_effect","proposed_value":"None/Not a major inducer or inhibitor","reason":"FDA/NCBI sources contradict CYP inhibitor classification.","requires_user_approval":true,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548304/"]},{"current_value":"QT prolongation not typical; urinary/retinal toxicity drove withdrawal","field":"qt_interval_effect","proposed_value":"QT prolongation possible; FDA label reports mean 7.7-msec QT prolongation at 400 mg TID and recommends monitoring in at-risk patients. Global discontinuation was attributed by GSK/EMA to limited use/commercial reasons, not efficacy or safety.","reason":"FDA label supports QT effect; ILAE/GSK and EMA contradict safety-driven withdrawal wording.","requires_user_approval":true,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf","https://www.ilae.org/files/dmfile/GSK_RetigabineTrobalt-Reminder.pdf","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/public-statement/public-statement-trobalt-withdrawal-marketing-authorisation-european-union_en.pdf"]},{"current_value":"EMA Trobalt withdrawal page; Sills and Rogawski 2020 ASM mechanism review","field":"mechanism_source","proposed_value":"EMA Trobalt EPAR summary; Sills and Rogawski 2020 ASM mechanism review; NCBI LiverTox Ezogabine","reason":"The withdrawal page supports market withdrawal, not mechanism; EMA EPAR and NCBI LiverTox support the mechanism.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/overview/trobalt-epar-summary-public_en.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32120063/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548304/"]},{"current_value":"EMA/UK SmPC","field":"mechanism_source_tier","proposed_value":"EMA EPAR/peer-reviewed review","reason":"No UK SmPC source was verified in the cited row sources; the verified source tier is EMA EPAR plus peer-reviewed/NCBI evidence.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/overview/trobalt-epar-summary-public_en.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32120063/"]},{"current_value":"NCBI LiverTox anticonvulsants table; EMA Trobalt withdrawal page; ILAE/GSK discontinuation notice; Sills and Rogawski 2020 ASM mechanism review; FDA/DailyMed labeling","field":"evidence_sources","proposed_value":"FDA Potiga prescribing information; FDA Potiga Drug Safety Communication; NCBI LiverTox Ezogabine; EMA Trobalt EPAR summary and withdrawal statement; ILAE/GSK Trobalt/Potiga discontinuation notice; Sills and Rogawski 2020 ASM mechanism review; cited PubMed RCTs","reason":"Names trusted sources that actually support the populated cells and removes DailyMed as a black-box source.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf","https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-safety-and-availability/fda-drug-safety-communication-fda-determines-2013-labeling-adequate-manage-risk-retinal","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548304/","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/overview/trobalt-epar-summary-public_en.pdf","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/public-statement/public-statement-trobalt-withdrawal-marketing-authorisation-european-union_en.pdf","https://www.ilae.org/files/dmfile/GSK_RetigabineTrobalt-Reminder.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32120063/"]},{"current_value":"2011 FDA approval; EMA Trobalt/retigabine marketing authorisation granted 2011 and withdrawn 2018","field":"year_fda_cleared","proposed_value":"2011 FDA approval; EMA Trobalt/retigabine marketing authorisation granted 28 Mar 2011 and withdrawn 19 Jul 2018","reason":"Adds exact EMA authorization and withdrawal dates from EMA withdrawal statement.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/public-statement/public-statement-trobalt-withdrawal-marketing-authorisation-european-union_en.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf"]}],"rct_link_checks":[{"label":"Lim2016","pmid":"27376872","proposed_action":"Retain link.","rationale":"PubMed record is for retigabine/ezogabine adjunctive therapy in adult Asian patients with drug-resistant partial-onset seizures; title identifies randomized, placebo-controlled Phase III study. ClinicalTrials.gov NCT01648101 confirms randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled Phase III design with 600/900 mg retigabine versus placebo.","status":"verified","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27376872/"},{"label":"French2011","pmid":"21451152","proposed_action":"Retain link.","rationale":"PubMed record is the RESTORE1 randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial of ezogabine/retigabine 1200 mg/day in partial epilepsy. ClinicalTrials.gov NCT00232596 confirms randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled Phase 3 design.","status":"verified","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21451152/"},{"label":"Brodie2010","pmid":"20944074","proposed_action":"Retain link; treat deterministic title-language warning as a false positive after manual review.","rationale":"PubMed/NCBI LiverTox and FDA statistical review identify this as RESTORE2/Study 302, a multicenter randomized double-blind placebo-controlled Phase III trial of ezogabine/retigabine 600 and 900 mg/day for refractory partial epilepsy. ClinicalTrials.gov NCT00235755 also confirms Phase 3 randomized placebo-controlled design.","status":"verified","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20944074/"},{"label":"Porter2007","pmid":"17420403","proposed_action":"Retain link.","rationale":"PubMed/NCBI LiverTox identifies the Porter 2007 dose-ranging retigabine trial for partial-onset seizures. FDA statistical review identifies Study 205 as Phase IIb, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled, multicenter, dose-ranging retigabine 600/900/1200 mg/day versus placebo.","status":"verified","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17420403/"}],"source_updates":[{"current_value":"FDA/DailyMed search on 05-20-2026: no current label found for terms [ezogabine; retigabine; Potiga; Trobalt].","field":"fda_black_box_warning_source","proposed_value":"FDA Potiga prescribing information, May 2016, and FDA Drug Safety Communication, 06-16-2015: boxed warning for retinal abnormalities and potential vision loss identified for Potiga (ezogabine).","reason":"FDA sources directly support a boxed warning; DailyMed is not permissible for this field.","requires_user_approval":true,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf","https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-safety-and-availability/fda-drug-safety-communication-fda-determines-2013-labeling-adequate-manage-risk-retinal"]},{"current_value":"NCBI LiverTox anticonvulsants table; EMA Trobalt withdrawal page; ILAE/GSK discontinuation notice; Sills and Rogawski 2020 ASM mechanism review; FDA/DailyMed labeling","field":"evidence_sources","proposed_value":"FDA Potiga prescribing information; FDA Potiga Drug Safety Communication; NCBI LiverTox Ezogabine; EMA Trobalt EPAR summary and withdrawal statement; ILAE/GSK Trobalt/Potiga discontinuation notice; Sills and Rogawski 2020 ASM mechanism review; cited PubMed RCTs","reason":"Improves source specificity and removes DailyMed for black-box support.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf","https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-safety-and-availability/fda-drug-safety-communication-fda-determines-2013-labeling-adequate-manage-risk-retinal","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548304/","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/overview/trobalt-epar-summary-public_en.pdf","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/public-statement/public-statement-trobalt-withdrawal-marketing-authorisation-european-union_en.pdf","https://www.ilae.org/files/dmfile/GSK_RetigabineTrobalt-Reminder.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32120063/"]},{"current_value":"EMA Trobalt withdrawal page; Sills and Rogawski 2020 ASM mechanism review","field":"mechanism_source","proposed_value":"EMA Trobalt EPAR summary; Sills and Rogawski 2020 ASM mechanism review; NCBI LiverTox Ezogabine","reason":"EMA EPAR, not EMA withdrawal page, supports the mechanism cell.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/overview/trobalt-epar-summary-public_en.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32120063/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548304/"]}],"summary":"Ezogabine/retigabine row mostly verifies for aliases, availability, dosing, formulation, metabolism, focal-seizure indication, and RCT outcome calculations. Updates are needed for FDA boxed warning status, source wording, enzyme filter, QT/withdrawal wording, and mechanism source specificity. The four PubMed RCT links should be retained; Brodie2010 is a valid Phase III placebo-controlled RCT despite the deterministic warning."}
